# Git Commit Messages

Follow [Conventional Commits v1.0.0](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/en/v1.0.0/).

## Required format

```
<type>(<scope>): <description>

- <filename>: <what changed in this file>
- <filename>: <what changed in this file>
```

## Subject

- **feat** — new feature (MINOR in SemVer)
- **fix** — bug fix (PATCH in SemVer)
- Other allowed types: build, chore, ci, docs, style, refactor, perf, test, revert
- Scope: optional logical area (header, movies, ui, apps, projects, router, api)
- Description: imperative mood, lowercase, no trailing period
- Breaking changes: `type(scope)!: description` or `BREAKING CHANGE:` footer

## Body (project convention)

- Blank line after subject
- One bullet per materially changed file
- Format: `- filename: short explanation`
- Use **filename only** — never include directory paths unless disambiguating duplicate names
- Wrong: `- .githooks/commit-msg: ...` or `- .cursor/rules/git-commits.mdc: ...`
- Correct: `- commit-msg: ...` or `- git-commits.mdc: ...`
- Use **full path** only when multiple changed files share the same name
- Order by importance; skip lockfiles unless they are the main change

## Example

```
chore(rules): refine commit message formatting guidelines

- .cursorrules: update formatting rules to clarify bullet point structure
- git-commits.mdc: adjust examples to reflect new bullet point format
- commit-msg: modify regex to enforce new bullet point format
```

## Example with duplicate filenames

```
feat(pages): update shared layout components

- src/pages/Movies/Movies.tsx: add pagination state
- src/pages/Apps/Apps.tsx: add app grid layout
```

Never commit `.env`, credentials, or secrets.
